{"id": 4397153, "name": "Hill v. Burgess", "name_abbreviation": "Hill v. Burgess", "decision_date": "1892-09-29", "docket_number": "No. 3010", "first_page": "604", "last_page": "605", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "37 S.C. 604"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "34 Conn., 398", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Conn.", "case_ids": [507760], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/conn/34/0398-01"]}, {"cite": "14 S. C., 90", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 187, "char_count": 2089, "ocr_confidence": 0.465, "pagerank": {"raw": 4.03580807328026e-08, "percentile": 0.14963575096463153}, "sha256": "d386dc17f032ef262e06becbe8a9e1d8a5af24e16adda02aabf47eaf121ffd3e", "simhash": "1:45b4488b5cd8695e", "word_count": 356}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T22:43:47.968146+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["Hill v. Burgess."], "opinions": [{"text": "Opinion by\nMr. Justice McGowan,\nThis was an action commenced in a trial justice\u2019s court for the recovery of some lumber. The lumber was purchased by defendant at a sale made by a sawyer, after due advertisement, under authority conferred in an agreement made by one Bullock, tenant of plaintiff, with the sawyer, without plaintiff\u2019s knowledge or consent. Before the sale, defendant had notice of plaintiff\u2019s claim to the lumber. Judgment for plaintiff was, on appeal to the Circuit Court, affirmed. Fraser, J. Defendant appealed.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice McGowan,"}], "attorneys": ["W. H. Martin and F. P. McGowan, for appellant.", "Ball, Simians & Ball, contra."], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "No. 3010.\nHill v. Burgess.\nApril Term, 1892.\nW. H. Martin and F. P. McGowan, for appellant.\nBall, Simians & Ball, contra.\nThe court said: '\u201cThe timber was the property of the plaintiff. Her tenant (Bullock) had it cut, hauled, and sawed into lumber without her knowledge or consent.. We agree with the Circuit Judge, that this conduct of the tenant, without the knowledge of the plaintiff, could uot estop the plaintiff from asserting her right to the property wherever found. As a rule, estoppel does not arise from the conduct of others, but from that of the party sought to be estopped.\n\u201cWe also concur with the judge, that the defendant Burgess cannot be considered as a purchaser for value without notice, for, in fact, he had notice of plaintiff\u2019s claim before he sold the property. Besides, the plea of purchaser-for valuable consideration is purely equitable, and cannot stand against the legal title. See Lynch v. Hancock, 14 S. C., 90.\n\u201cNor do we think that Brown, the sawyer, could, as against Mrs. Hill, the owner, set up any mechanic\u2019s or other lien stipulated for by agreement with Bullock, who was not the legal owner of the property, and could make no binding agreement about- it. \u2018In order to charge a chattel with a mechanic\u2019s lien, the labor for which the lien is claimed must have been done at the request of the owner, or under circumstances from which his assent can be reasonably implied.\u2019 13 Am. & Eng. Enc. Law, 591, and see cases in the notes, especially that of Clark v. Hale, 34 Conn., 398.\u201d\nJudgment affirmed.\nSeptember 29, 1892."}, "file_name": "0604-02", "first_page_order": 612, "last_page_order": 613}